  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n- Warm and Dry Today, Rain Showers Sunday Afternoon/Evening\n\nA second day in a row of highs in the mid 60s (20F above normal)\nis in store for the region with sunny skies (a bit cooler near the\nlake). This is not quite record setting but we are coming close!\nRecords are in the upper 60s to around 70. A deepening upper\ntrough approaches the region on Sunday, pushing a surface cold\nfront through during the day. The front may be held back enough to\nallow highs to once again reach the 60s especially near and east\nof US 131.\n\nTemperatures aloft at 850mb look to be around +10C ahead of the\ncold front on Sunday, then drop considerably to around -10C Monday\nnight. Frontal rain showers are likely on Sunday, though mainly \nlight showers are expected. Thunder is currently not forecast \ngiven almost zero MUCAPE. Rain showers likely begin midday to \nearly afternoon and end during the evening.\n\n- Lake Effect Snow Showers Possible North of I-96 Late Monday\n\nA brief window for some lake effect snow showers exists late\nMonday afternoon into Monday night mainly north of I-96. Odds are\nlooking a bit better that at least some light snow showers may\noccur in this region, particularly closer to US 10. With 850mb\ntemperatures dropping into the -8C to -10C range Monday evening\nand Monday night, that will be cold enough to support some lake\neffect generation. GFS and RRFS Bufkit soundings for Ludington \nand Big Rapids suggests there may be enough moisture within the\nDGZ to get snow development (ie. the RRFS at LDM has 100% RH in\nthe DGZ Monday afternoon into evening), though RH drops off below\nthe DGZ with some evaporation likely to occur. So, while flakes\nmay be developing 5000-8000 ft aloft, dry air in the sfc-2000 ft\nlayer may reduce what falls and makes it to the ground.\n\nSurface temperatures steadily drop below freezing after sunset\nMonday evening, so if snow overcomes the drier layer near the\nground it's not impossible a light coating could develop on lesser\ntraveled roads. Overall, this looks like a minor situation but\nwill continue monitoring trends.\n\n- 20% Probability for Rain/Snow Mix Wednesday into Thanksgiving\n\nEnsemble guidance favors upper troughing to return by the middle\nof next week. Normally this would bring some interesting weather\nto the region, but the bulk of ensemble membership favors surface\nlow development to occur across the eastern U.S. with a possible\nNor'easter on Thanksgiving. Only around 20% of the guidance\nsupports a glancing blow of a rain/snow mix to impact mainly\nsouthern and southeast Lower MI Wednesday into Thanksgiving.",
